Fri Oct 16 19:45:00 UTC 2026 - tokajer <tokajer@tokajer.at>

- Waydroid: Android ran in UTC. The install now sets
  persist.sys.timezone from the system time zone (before the first
  Android start; an existing Android data folder keeps its zone)
- Drives: NTFS drives added for automatic mounting failed with
  "unknown filesystem type 'ntfs3'": suse-module-tools blacklists the
  driver, which also blocks the fs-ntfs3 alias used by mount. Adding an
  NTFS drive now links /etc/modprobe.d/60-blacklist_fs-ntfs3.conf to
  /dev/null, like suse-module-tools' own unblacklist script does when
  run in a terminal
- "Remove NazoOS kernel" ended with zypper 107: the kernel's cert-postun
  runs "mokutil --delete --root-pw" (yescrypt problem, ADR 0024).
  efivarfs is mounted read-only for the removal, the script skips; the
  key stays enrolled
- Boot menu: while the NazoOS kernel is the default, a top-level entry
  "NazoOS (openSUSE kernel)" is added and set as GRUB's fallback
  (/etc/grub.d/15_nazoos_fallback). Secure Boot without the enrolled
  key ("bad shim loader signature") now starts the openSUSE kernel by
  itself instead of stopping at "Failed to boot both default and
  fallback entries"
- Kernel page: says "enroll the key first" instead of "restart" when
  Secure Boot is on and the key is missing or waiting
- Key password dialog: the text field gets the keyboard focus (Enter
  and Tab reached the window behind it)
- "Use openSUSE kernel" started kernel-nazoos again: it set the first
  boot menu entry, which boots the newest kernel of any flavor
- Boot menu: the top entry "NazoOS" now starts the chosen kernel
  (GRUB_TOP_LEVEL) and is the default, instead of a highlighted
  "Advanced options" submenu. Kept up to date after every kernel
  install/removal by a file trigger on /usr/lib/modules and by
  nazoos-kernel-boot.service (no condition on kernel-prefer any more,
  the way back needs it too); removing kernel-nazoos drops it again

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Fri Oct 16 18:30:00 UTC 2026 - tokajer <tokajer@tokajer.at>

- Secure Boot key (ADR 0024): MokManager password comes from a dialog
  (suggested "nazoos", 4-16 letters or digits; shows what to type on a
  German keyboard, where Z and Y are swapped in MokManager), no longer
  the root password. Calamares
  writes yescrypt hashes, "mokutil --root-pw" cannot use them: the
  kernel's cert-post failed, zypper ended with 107 and the kernel
  install stopped before the boot entry and boot counter
- Kernel install on UEFI: download first, take the certificate out of
  the package and queue it for MokManager before installing, so the
  kernel's own cert-post skips it (also with Secure Boot off)
- Key card also shown with Secure Boot off; "Enroll key" asks for the
  password too
- Key card showed "not enrolled" while the key waited for MokManager:
  MokNew is readable only by root, the helper now leaves a marker in
  /var/lib/nazoos that counts until the next boot
- Requires cpio and openssl

